    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Median Empire grew from a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> tribes into a leading Near Eastern power ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>.</li><li>Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#medes">Medes</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assur">Ashur</a> in 614 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nineveh">Nineveh</a> in 612 BCE.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> absorbed it c. 550 BCE, but its nobles and court customs lived on inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Median influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> ran so deep that Greek writers often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persians">Persians</a> '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#medes">Medes</a>' and termed collaboration with Persia 'Medism'.</p></aside>
    </section>

